1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:07,140
In Montricoux, timber-frame construction was linked to a kind of demographic boom during the 15th century,

2
00:00:07,380 --> 00:00:12,300
which came after several crises throughout the 14th century.

3
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:15,440
So in the 15th century, people began returning.

4
00:00:16,240 --> 00:00:19,780
Many people came from the Rouergue region and from the Massif Central,

5
00:00:20,440 --> 00:00:22,420
areas that had been somewhat less affected,

6
00:00:22,860 --> 00:00:26,420
especially because Quercy had been a frontier zone between the English and the French.

7
00:00:26,920 --> 00:00:28,660
As a result, it had become quite depopulated.

8
00:00:29,220 --> 00:00:32,320
So most newcomers came from neighboring regions.

9
00:00:33,240 --> 00:00:38,240
And Montricoux had a rather attractive incentive for new arrivals.

10
00:00:38,350 --> 00:00:46,180
There was a clause stating that the inhabitants of Montricoux had the right to take wood in order to build their houses

11
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:51,340
and to make their utensils, tools, wine barrels, and so on.

12
00:00:52,340 --> 00:00:55,500
It was almost like putting up a sign saying “free building materials.”

13
00:00:56,820 --> 00:01:00,200
It was truly meant to attract people.

14
00:01:01,340 --> 00:01:06,100
This probably explains, at least in part, the success of timber-frame architecture,

15
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:09,040
since wood was essentially a free resource.

16
00:01:09,440 --> 00:01:13,000
We can also observe that in much of the 15th-century timber-frame architecture,

17
00:01:13,460 --> 00:01:14,640
the infill was made of earth.

18
00:01:15,300 --> 00:01:20,700
Likewise, the earth was probably free or extremely inexpensive.

19
00:01:20,860 --> 00:01:25,920
What became interesting while studying the timber framing in Montricoux

20
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:32,820
is that the material itself reflects the landscape from which it was taken.

21
00:01:33,140 --> 00:01:39,280
What we see in the 15th century are trees that grew quite straight and evenly.

22
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:45,360
We can see that they were often between fifty and one hundred years old, meaning they had time to mature.

23
00:01:45,760 --> 00:01:50,840
Oak takes time to grow if you want fine pieces of timber.

24
00:01:51,440 --> 00:01:55,840
This gives us a reflection of a landscape with probably well-managed forests.

25
00:01:56,020 --> 00:01:59,180
It was not wild forest — in the Middle Ages, forests were carefully managed.

26
00:01:59,420 --> 00:02:06,180
By contrast, in the 18th century, the wood being used became very different, much more knotted.

27
00:02:06,460 --> 00:02:09,479
We begin to see curved shapes and more branch junctions.

28
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:17,120
This reflects a very different use of resources and a landscape that had become much more open.

29
00:02:17,680 --> 00:02:19,720
A landscape more similar to hedged countryside.

30
00:02:19,900 --> 00:02:24,400
We also know that in the modern era there was extensive charcoal production.

31
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:30,700
This strongly impacted the forest landscape because instead of preserving timber for construction,

32
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:38,380
forests were exploited for charcoal production and firewood, which were more profitable.

33
00:02:38,540 --> 00:02:45,100
Through all this, we are able to speak more broadly about society itself,

34
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:50,220
and about how people came to build these houses using those materials.